Date: Wed, 1 Dec 93 2:01:13 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

Mark Shewmaker describes a detailed transaction scenario for how the
"MOF1CE" might be paid for, and why Mark will not lend out his book in
order to make sure he gets the $10 held in escrow for 5 years.

Lots of detail, but I think the main point is being missed
(interesting how the passive voice--"is being missed"--is used here by
me to avoid the appearance of rudeness to Mark...but I digress).

The main danger is that someone like the "Information Liberation
Front" will file off the serial numbers as best they can and then
widely distribute the book.

(Nick Szabo mentioned being willing to pay $200 for a CD-ROM with all
of the writings of his 50 favorite authors. And go to Taiwan or Hong
Kong to see classic American textbooks being sold for $2.00 or
less--on cheap paper, poor type, and no royalties.)

Given that it only takes one (or a handful, if you buy the
calculations reported recently) "defector" to "liberate" the book, no
amount of escrow that an individual can afford will be enough.
Bluntly, a $10 escrow or even a $200 escrow is not going to make any
difference to a liberator.

The threat of punishment might, provided the punishment has an
effective cost (probability of being caught x punishment) that is
large, at least larger than the expected gains.

(And the expected monetary gain by liberating David's MOF1CE may not
be that great--no offence, David--because of course the liberator
cannot stop _others_ from making copies. I suspect many liberations of
texts will be for ideological reason. Wanting to get info out fast and
cheaply. Not such a bad idea.)

Whenever I worry overmuch about the end of copyrights, I imagine an
alternate world in which using the arguments or ideas of another is a
violation of intellectual property laws. (A radical libertarian sect
known as the "Galombosians" believed something quite like this.) It
might be argued in this other world that originators of ideas need to
be "incentivized" and "rewarded" for their efforts by granting them
claim for some period of time, with the soldiers of the state
available to enforce the claim. 

In our world, we freely borrow ideas and concepts, though not precise
words (plagiarism), and we think this is a Good Thing. I expect in 50
years we will look back on copyrights the same way. Knowledge that is
active knowhow, active agents, will not be so easily copyable and
_this_ will be the valuable knowledge. 

Several others have recently made a similar point, and I think they
are of course right. 

And people will have to learn the hard way that if they want something
kept secret, they'll have to keep it secret themselves. Whether
central law or polycentric/PPL law, entropy wins: once the horses are
out of the barn, they're hard to put back in.

Big changes are coming.

(And being the founding member of the "Information Liberation Front"
over on Cypherpunks, about a year ago, I can tell you the changes are
happening already and are not just theoretical nonsense.)

...Now, where was I in my scan and OCR of Bruce Schneier's overpriced
new book, "Applied Cryptography"?

--Tim May

(Just kidding about liberating Schneier's book! Even at $44.95 for a
paperback, it's worth buying. And the ILF has so far only scanned-in
relatively short academic papers, like Chaum's "Dining Cryptographers"
paper. These are nonprofit items anyway. Longer works, or those with
lots of equations, figures, etc., are just not worth scanning, at
least not for free and for a limited audience. But technology will
change, with better OCR and higher-resolution displays.)


