From: mlshew@netcom.com (Mark Shewmaker)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

Timothy C. May points out some perceived problems with my transaction
scenario for "MOF1CE", but I believe a point is being missed. :-)

>The main danger is that someone like the "Information Liberation
>Front" will file off the serial numbers as best they can and then
>widely distribute the book.

>Bluntly, a $10 escrow or even a $200 escrow is not going to make any
>difference to a liberator.

Answer number 1:

Then don't worry about it.  Worry about the profit you can control
and maximize, but not the part that will disappear no matter what
you do.

If a $10 or $0.50, or $1 escrow with a lottery is what gets you your
profits, go for it, but don't worry about might-have-beens that are
really could-never-have-beens.  Donate to the SPA, ruffle your
feathers, make a lot of noise, but don't loose sleep over a problem
you can't control.

What if top marketing analysts at wordperfect discovered some new
sales method, that would increase the sales of their products by 70%
overnight, sustained for at least the next ten years, but would also
increase the rate of piracy of their products by 500%, for the same
length of time.

Should they forget about the plan because due to some misplaced
morals, they fear that they are contributing to the delinquency of
the citizenry at large?

Or should they go for the money?

Answer number 2:  (Warning--I ramble and repeat--not enough sleep)

The fact that some people may or will receive some benefits of
either WordPerfect's or David Friedman's works in less than
legitimate ways is, in and of itself, unimportant to the economics.

What is important is that there is a group of software providers
willing to write for money, and a group of customers willing to pay
money for software.  The twain shall meet, ILF or no ILF.

Most every piece of software can be gotten 'free' for less work than
actually paying for it, _today_.  Yet people still buy the stuff.

Even in a full cryptoanarcy and anarchocapitalist society that
doesn't have a single PPL that respects intellectual property
outside of specific contracts, as long as there are people willing
to pay (enough) for the stuff anyway, there will be people who will
sell it.

If they can buy their Rocky XVII through their tv for fifty cents
directly from MGM (?), or for a quarter through anonymous remailers,
some percentage of them will buy it direct.

This isn't logic, it's marketing.

More rambling:

Imagine all the people who'd like to buy a version of a certain work
of software that the author would consider legitimate.  Imagine
people who are standing on the fence about it, but would purchase it
with some incentive.  (Easy payment method + slight inducement to
keep the agreement over the next year or so.)

When I imagine both sets of people at once, one thing I cannot
simultaneously imagine is the failure of all attempts to match them
up with the software producers.

What I proposed was one (inefficient) method to match them up that
would imply that software publishers could still make money.

It's not by any means perfect, but it's a proof of concept.  I'm
sure that other people can think of solutions orders of magnitude
better, but for now, this is enough for me to feel confident that
even in a cryptoanarchy I'll be able to get updates to my compiler.

Making the payment method a convenient and self-enforcing short term
non-disclosure agreement (effectively with "fair-use" provisions)
can only increase the publisher's incomes over what they otherwise
would have been, and even if neither figure can ever be obtained in
the real world, those are two income data points that would be
useful to know when figuring out how to do business, _not_ all the
profits that you could have made if things were only like they were
fifty years ago.

