Date: Wed, 1 Dec 93 7:04:10 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Business ideas

Interesting business ideas on the "Random Thoughts" thread!
Here at a somewhat higher level of abstraction, are ideas for 
new kinds of business entities:

* Anonymous consulting.  Work out scheme(s) for on the one hand,
finding consultants and paying them, and on the other hand providing
these information services and getting paid for them, 
without either party knowing the identity of another.
Recently appeared a new kind of business called a "software
escrow service" that serves as an intermediary between 
software consultants and their customers.  This might be
extended to mediate between anonymous parties and serve other 
kinds of information services.

* Multinational small business.  Currently the vast majority
of worldwide businesses are small, but the vast bulk of multinational
business is large corporations.  In the future the size distribution
of multinationals will approach that of local business. The phase 
change between these states may be quite rapid as telecomm
and transport costs pass through a "triple point".  Barriers to 
multinational small business:

	(1) legal: prohibitively complex maze of jurisdictions
	(2) language/culture
	(3) telecomm costs
	(4) transport costs

Both (3) and (4) have become unprecedentedly low, and with fiber
optics (3) will drop orders of magnitude further.  Small business
can cut through the Gordian knot of (1) with the technology 
of jurisdictional independence, aka crypto-anarchy: strong
encryption, foiling of traffic analysis with steganography and mixes,
etc.  For small business use these capabilities must be made
seamlessly available in their telecommunications software.
Once that is done, small business has nearly endless opportunities
to arbitrage between the relative strengths and weaknesses of
various localities in various aspects of their business, much
like multinationals currently take advantage of relaxed environmental
standards and cheap labor costs in Third World countries now.
Vast markets may be opened by short-circuiting the intervention
of governments in both macro (eg industrial production) and micro
(eg banning drugs, porn, gambling, etc.) economic decisions, by 
short-circuiting the confiscation of over $3 trillion a year out 
of the world economy by various governments, etc.

(2), language and culture, may remain the largest barrier.  Two methods 
of attack:

* The rise of virtual nations.  Multinational small businesses
might speak entirely English, Japanese, Mandarin, etc.  Their
employees might live primarily within a single cultural mileau,
dispersed thru a large number of small ethnic communities around 
the world, keeping close culture-specific, multimedia communciations 
links between the communities.  The worldwide Anglo, Japanese, and 
Mandarin business networks will be taken up by other cultures, and 
decentralized in general.

* Language-translation software.  Crude, specialized versions
might soon be good enough for many kinds of small business uses, such
as translating technical manuals.

* English becoming increasingly the _de facto_ language for
business and technology.

One interesting question is what small changes might bring about the 
phase change faster.  Something as simple as a user-freindly voice
teleconferencing system, might be enough to tip the penguins into the 
water.  Or it might be widespread Internet access with encrypted, 
route-mixed TCP/IP connections and virtual whiteboards.

Another interesting question in any major new trend is what will be the
bottleneck pieces.  With the clonable PC, Intel's CPUs and
Microsoft's DOS turned out to be the only proprietary elements,
with assembly, disk drives, terminals, etc. highly competitive.
(I predict the winner of the current PowerPC/Pentium/workstation
wars will also be easily clonable, even if it's otherwise 
technologically inferior).  What will be the bottleneck
tools of the coming explosion in multinational small business?

