Okay, I recently sent an update on activity here in the east. What are you
guys up to out west? How come I never hear from you any more?

Is there an Agorics FAQ? If not, I nominate the following for rev. 0.
Please feel free to send me (tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu) additions about who you
are and what you're doing that might relate to Agorics.


Here is a brief introduction to the persons involved in Agorics.

  PROF. DON LAVOIE
Most of Don's time has been taken up by his duties as chair of a new
department, the Program on Social and Organizational Learning. He's hiring
faculty and setting up curricula, etc. He is co-director of the Agorics
Project. He is promoting hypertext in GMU.

  Dr. HOWARD BAETJER
Howard recently defended his dissertation with success. His dissertation
was on the topic of software as capital. He explored the problems and
opportunities presented by the easy copyability of information goods. He is
working at PSOL on something related to Agorics. He was one of the first to
become interested in Agorics at GMU.

  Dr. BRAD COX
Brad a well-known software engineer interested in market issues concerning
software reuse. He's trying to change the way software is produced and
distributed by promoting a pay-per-use method made prctical by hardware
developed recently.

  KEVIN LACOBIE
Kevin is working on a dissertation involving computer modelling. He is not
using GAs, but is influenced by Alife/ adaptive agents.

  BILL TULLOH
Bill started all this by getting Don and Bill interested in some articles
by computer types (Miller and Drexler) on using market concepts and
institutions within AI software. He's working on a dissertation on the
economics of software design, drawing a parallel between market
institutions and design approaches.

  DAVE BURNS
I'm working on my dissertation, which looks to be an extension of Axelrod's
prisoners' dilemma tournament using genetic algorithms. I somehow relate
this to policy making, Hayek's critique of constructivist rationalism, and
various aspects of Buchanan's Constitutional Political Economy. 

  HADON NASH
Hadon is a computer science masters student who thinks he has made a
conceptual breakthrough on Agoric computation. The basic idea is to improve
the performance of classifier systems by making the bucket brigade more
market-like.

All the above are at George Mason University. On the West coast there are
three others (at least).

  MARK MILLER and ERIC DREXLER
Mark is a software engineer working on hypertext systems at Xanadu Corp. He
co-wrote two papers with Eric Drexler about using market concepts within
software.

  DEAN TRIBBLE
Is working on a computer language that would have primitives for using
market-like mechanisms in software.

Several projects have sprung up around the Agorics theme.

THE AGORICS PROJECT
This is an academic research effort exploring the interdisciplinary borders
between Economics and Computer Science. Co-directors are Don Lavoie and
Mark Miller. It is funded through the Center for the Study of Market
Processes (I think). We used to get together for weekly meetings, but
things have gotten too diffuse. Everyone is working on their own projects
now, though administratively the Agorics Project continues. 

Miller & Drexler wrote their papers on using markets within computer
systems (especially in large open networks). Bill saw these papers and got
Don and Howie interested. They went out to California to talk to Miller and
Drexler about these ideas. They came back with four interesting potential
topics: the unusual aspects of the software industry, the potential impact
of hypertext for education, use of new computer techniques (especially GAs)
for economic modelling, and the economic issues in large system design.
Each of these issues continues to be addressed, but we've split them up in
a spontaneous division of labor.

Bill, Don, and Howie co-wrote a paper on the basic inspiration for THE
MARKET PROCESS, and they also received some grants to do a study of the
software industry. That study is complete but not yet available to the
public.

AGORIC ENTERPRISES
This firm was started by Bill, Kevin, and Howie to provide consulting to
software firms interested in these issues. Howie has since reduced his role
here to concentrate on UUC and his duties at PSOL.

Userights Utility Company (UUC)
This group is pursueing Brad's idea of pay-per-use software. We're
exploring the social issues, writing a specification, and working on a
prototype of the software.

THE AGORA
Howie stared this project and Kevin is continueing it. It is a simulation
environment.

